Like you're, you're right. Like the, well, there was plenty with gameplay with like you pick up the, uh, the slingshot at one point and you got to kind of count your ammo and things like that.

00:25:02.770 --> 00:25:07.150
And then you get the boomerang and you get like different weapons and stuff that changed it a little bit as you went.

00:25:07.150 --> 00:25:11.990
Um, but also just the fact that there were a lot of environments.

00:25:11.990 --> 00:25:18.110
There's a lot, there's like, there's the bridge that you go across and you've got those skull things that steal your items.

00:25:18.270 --> 00:25:20.250
They steal your boomerang, right?

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:30.150
Yeah, they'll take the boomerang. And then you have, then you have like the, the ice world, which has like the very, I mean, the very overused, but like slippery physics of the ground.

00:25:30.610 --> 00:25:34.670
And like little things like that were interesting.

00:25:34.890 --> 00:25:45.210
I mean, it never got like crazy different, but I think that the, the idea of you have your map and you're exploring, you gotta remember, oh, and I gotta like kind of, I think Sean said this earlier, like, oh, now I gotta go back to that ice world that I saw earlier.

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:54.190
And it, I think it, it more so rather than it all changing, it's like you learn the layout of everything and then you feel like you're actually exploring this like big environment.

00:25:54.190 --> 00:26:09.930
Yeah, I think that the, the palette swaps help, but the individual screens aren't, um, I don't find them as memorable as they should be for good, uh, for, for good exploration landmarks in a game like this.

00:26:10.030 --> 00:26:19.770
And also the game has this whole front and back system, uh, where there are two maps where you can go between front and back worlds, I guess.

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:26.830
And it just labels it on your, if you hit the start button, you'll get to the map screen and that just like labels whether you're on the front or back of the map.

00:26:27.210 --> 00:26:28.410
I have no, very, yeah.

00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:32.850
I have no spatial like, uh, like boundaries for that.

00:26:33.010 --> 00:26:42.510
Well, wasn't that, didn't, correct me if I'm wrong, but didn't that, didn't you switch from the front to the back by going through, going all the way through one of the first person rooms, right?

00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:44.330
I guess. And then you come out on the other side.

00:26:44.330 --> 00:26:45.510
That's exactly how you did it.

00:26:45.510 --> 00:26:51.750
But I guess like, I don't know if this is what Sean was trying to say, but yeah, I'm glad that the whole thing isn't just a giant warehouse.

00:26:51.750 --> 00:27:02.550
I'm glad that it's changed by areas, but there's not enough going on or difference in, in the way that each individual screen looks that I'm able to remember like, oh, that's right.

00:27:02.550 --> 00:27:07.450
I'm on floor three right now of the front or, you know, or I'm in the back now.

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:14.670
You know, I was able to tell like, yeah, I'm in the part where, you know, there's ice on the floor or, oh, I'm in the, the, what would I call it?

00:27:14.730 --> 00:27:15.830
Like the caverns or.

